# A base constructor makes the object of its construction in its own body: an allocation of the class's own layout. A literal that it returns on one way is an allocation too, and is another object: `new Early(true)` is no Early.
# While every allocation that reached the result of a base constructor counted as the object of the construction, `new Early(..)` was proven to be an Early, `next` was entered with the literal as `this`, and the entry's check ended a right program: "an entry parameter is not of the type that the compiler has proven of it".
# RUN: link functions mechanisms=all
# CHECK: function f1 callers-known one-callee {{*}}result=L0|L1
# CHECK: function f3 callers-known one-callee {{*}}result=number|string
# CHECK-NEXT: parameter 0 {{*}}proven=L0|L1
